#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106808} :activity/id #uuid "b97eef31-cdcb-509f-a8e2-7468bee3daec" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049713 :topic/id #uuid "0d2ecf90-ccdc-56b7-9726-e90d7669ae2f" :topic/math-academy-id 1362} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106804 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105845 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105684 :question/id #uuid "ec8ac943-1880-5f2a-81c3-35c136126ec1" :question/math-academy-id "e-8328"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a91e7c54-7ba7-5ea2-99a1-80510e77ac88" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048192} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050125}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106536} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106542}] :knowledge-point/title "Basic Derivatives of the Hyperbolic Functions"} :step/id #uuid "287f42f8-ca6c-5b35-9c81-f905b16d1d70" :step/math-academy-id 31128 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106807}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106805 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105846 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105685 :question/id #uuid "3dcfc1f2-40ee-54b4-a937-0e101c7238d4" :question/math-academy-id "e-8329"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a2992a46-063e-589d-a7e9-129e9f97eee6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048813}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106538} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106540}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Hyperbolic Functions Using the Product Rule"} :step/id #uuid "ae60fdbc-8100-5f74-88dd-dc11838ae028" :step/math-academy-id 31129 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106806}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106806 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105847 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105686 :question/id #uuid "1da70111-4e82-5fe4-b4be-08d0cc6f1110" :question/math-academy-id "e-8330"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dbeb43f0-d94d-5ee0-b7fb-fbbe0a804cb7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048842}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106539} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106541} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503709}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Hyperbolic Functions Using the Quotient Rule"} :step/id #uuid "2a2959f5-bf52-5984-b73d-213061968d0f" :step/math-academy-id 31130 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106809}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106807 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105848 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105687 :question/id #uuid "192e6748-24e3-5688-87d9-cdf379e24101" :question/math-academy-id "e-8332"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0016464f-27c3-5f1b-a6d3-fe7b5ffb7fbd" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051084}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106537} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106543}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Hyperbolic Functions Using the Chain Rule"} :step/id #uuid "c2857b6c-e2ae-51ac-8fed-6318597083dc" :step/math-academy-id 31133 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106805}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106808 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107119 :tutorial/content "$$\n\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n$$\n\nSince the hyperbolic functions are defined in terms of exponential functions, we can easily calculate their derivatives.\n\nThe derivatives of the basic hyperbolic functions are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\sinh x) &= \\cosh x \\\\\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\cosh x) &= \\sinh x \\\\\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\tanh x) &= sech^{2} x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's use these results to find the derivative of the following function:\n\n$$\ny = \\sinh{x} - 2\\cosh{x}\n$$\n\nUsing the usual properties of derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\sinh x - 2\\cosh x) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\sinh x) + \\frac{d}{dx}(-2\\cosh x) \\\\\n&= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\sinh x) - 2\\frac{d}{dx}(\\cosh x) \\\\\n&= \\cosh x - 2\\sinh x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "df316238-1ead-50db-80ec-d47656e6acad" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5207 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "5afd4693-e1ad-5f9f-94cf-364eceabce7d" :step/math-academy-id 32691 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106804}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106809 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107120 :tutorial/content "$$\n\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits} %\n$$\n\nWe would like the prove the following result:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(\\sinh{x}) = \\cosh x\n$$\n\nTo do this, we revert to the definition of the hyperbolic sine:\n\n$$\n\\sinh x = \\dfrac{1}{2}(e^x-e^{-x})\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equation with respect to $x$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\sinh x) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{1}{2}(e^{x} - e^{-x})) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot \\frac{d}{dx}(e^{x} - e^{-x}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}(e^{x} + e^{-x}) \\\\\n&= \\cosh x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe proof for the derivative of the hyperbolic cosine is very similar. You may wish to try it for yourself." :tutorial/id #uuid "11e38932-d6a4-5649-bbbe-205fb1bad98d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5221 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Formula for the Derivative of the Hyperbolic Sine"} :step/id #uuid "e126b8c5-2d87-5d89-bbf3-8265d50ec279" :step/math-academy-id 32711 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106810}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106810 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107121 :tutorial/content "$$\n\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits} \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n$$\n\nWe would like the prove the following result:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x}(\\tanh{x}) = \\sech^2{x}\n$$\n\nWe do this using the definition of the hyperbolic tangent. The most convenient definition to work with for this purpose is\n\n$$\n\\tanh{x} = \\dfrac{e^{2x}-1}{e^{2x}+1}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above equation with respect to $x$ using the quotient rule, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\tanh x) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{e^{2x} - 1}{e^{2x} + 1}) \\\\\n&= ((e^{2x} - 1)^{′} \\cdot (e^{2x} + 1) - (e^{2x} - 1) \\cdot (e^{2x} + 1)^{′})/((e^{2x} + 1)^{2}) \\\\\n&= ((2e^{2x})(e^{2x} + 1) - (e^{2x} - 1)(2e^{2x}))/((e^{2x} + 1)^{2}) \\\\\n&= (2e^{4x} + 2e^{2x} - 2e^{4x} + 2e^{2x})/((e^{2x} + 1)^{2}) \\\\\n&= (4e^{2x})/((e^{2x} + 1)^{2}) \\\\\n&= (\\frac{2e^{x}}{e^{2x} + 1})^{2} \\\\\n&= (\\frac{2}{e^{x} + e^{-x}})^{2} \\\\\n&= sech^{2} x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2a7389d5-4ba5-5fd7-8edb-b983483e6099" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5223 :tutorial/title "Deriving the Formula for the Derivative of the Hyperbolic Tangent"} :step/id #uuid "0acbbc47-60c9-5d52-bac4-6df37c07940b" :step/math-academy-id 32713}] :activity/title "Differentiating Hyperbolic Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105995}]}
